.TH CUBATURES 3 "2026-10-10" "fortran_cubatures 0.2.0" "Fortran Library Functions"
.SH NAME
cubatures \- Gaussian cubature rules on finite element reference elements
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.B use cubatures
.B type(cubature) :: q
.B q = cubature(elm, degree)
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
Module
.B cubatures
provides the derived type
.BR cubature ,
the abscissae and weights of an integration rule on a reference element,
so that the integral of f is approximated by
.BR "sum(q%weights * f(q%abscissae))" .
Abscissae and weights are real64.
.SS Elements
.TP
.B CUB_LIN
line [\-1, 1], measure 2
.TP
.B CUB_TRI
unit triangle x, y >= 0, x + y <= 1, measure 1/2
.TP
.B CUB_QUA
quadrilateral [\-1, 1]^2, measure 4
.TP
.B CUB_TET
unit tetrahedron, measure 1/6
.TP
.B CUB_HEX
hexahedron [\-1, 1]^3, measure 8
.TP
.B CUB_WED
wedge: unit triangle x [\-1, 1], measure 1
.SS Constructor
.TP
.BR cubature ( elm ", " p )
Rule of degree of exactness p in every direction.
.TP
.BR cubature ( elm ", [" p1 ", " p2 ", ...])"
One degree per direction: [px, py] for quadrilaterals, [px, py, pz] for
hexahedra, [ptri, pz] for wedges.
.PP
Triangle and tetrahedron rules are exact for total degree p; tensor-product
rules for degree p_i in each coordinate; wedge rules for total degree ptri in
(x, y) times degree pz in z. All weights are positive and all points are
interior. There is no upper limit on the degree.
.SS Components
.BR elm ", " dim ", " degree(3) ", " npoints ,
.BR abscissae(dim,npoints) ", " weights(npoints) .
.SS Type-bound procedures
.TP
.BR "call q%set" ( elm ", " degree )
Build the rule in place, like the constructor.
.TP
.BR q%is_valid ()
True if the rule is set and consistent.
.TP
.BR "call q%summary" "([" unit "]), " "call q%show" "([" unit "])"
Write the element, dimension, degrees and number of points; show also
writes every point, weight and the weight sum.
.TP
.BR "call q%destroy" ()
Deallocate and reset.
.SH NOTES
An invalid element type, a negative degree or a degree array of the wrong
size stops the program with
.B error stop
and a message that starts with "cubature%set:".
Errors cannot be caught.
The only other stop, a Gauss\-Jacobi iteration that does not converge,
guards against an internal fault.
.PP
Tensor-product rules vary the first coordinate fastest; Gauss points in each
direction run from +1 down to \-1. Wedge rules vary the triangle point
fastest. Simplex abscissae are Cartesian coordinates, equal to the leading
barycentric coordinates.
.PP
Kinds are not exported; use real64 from iso_fortran_env. The module has no
state.
.SH EXAMPLE
.nf
use cubatures
type(cubature) :: q
q = cubature(CUB_TET, 4)
print *, q%npoints, sum(q%weights)   ! 27  0.16666666666666669
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
The complete reference is llms.txt in the source distribution and
https://willklausler.github.io/fortran_cubatures/
.SH AUTHOR
Will Klausler
